what's not to love when it comes to  lumpia crispy and flaky on the outside  juicy and tender on the inside it's  truly a party in every bite whether you  enjoy this popular filipino finger food  as a snack or main course lumpia is a  total crowd pleaser at any family  gathering so from our family to yours  we're excited to share our favorite way  of making lumpia the word lumpia  translates to spring roll in several  languages  it's evolved from many asian cultures so  there's a variety of ways to make it  traditional fried lumpia usually refers  to shanghai lumpia or lumpiang shanghai  it's the most popular version we also  think it's the easiest one to make  lumpiantoge or sometimes spelled toge or  lum pyang gulai is fried like lumpian  shanghai but the roll is bigger it's  served with white vinegar mixed with  garlic onions and black pepper the  filling usually consists of vegetables  like julian carrots bean sprouts sauteed  pork shrimp or both fresh lumpia also  known as lumpian saliva or lumpiang ubud  is not fried but wrapped in something  like a crib this version is typically  filled with meat but can easily be  vegetarian or vegan like this one it's  commonly served with peanut sauce and  topped with crushed peanuts now turon  isn't considered a version of lumpia by  everyone we think this can pass for  olympia but it's sweet a saba banana  which closely resembles a plantain  is wrapped in a lumpia wrapper with  jackfruit and sugar then fried you  probably guess which version we're  making today we're sticking with the  classic lumpian shanghai when it comes  to selecting a protein for our lumpiang  shanghai we tried pork  chicken beef and even plant-based meat  substitutes some people add shrimp to  the pork we love this combo so if you're  into it go for it just make sure to  coarsely chop the shrimp today we're  going with ground pork don't worry about  cooking your meat prior to wrapping if  your meat is pre-cooked it will be  harder to wrap because it won't bind  with the other ingredients  the meat will cook during frying the key  to a good filling are balanced flavors  and enough vegetables to keep our  protein moist and tender in our recipe  we're using carrots sweet onions and  water chestnuts water chestnuts are  going to give our lumpia a nice crunch  and moisture you can chop these  vegetables on your own or simply throw  them in a food processor and pulse  just don't puree them they should look  like this  toss this in a bowl with our ground pork  and add some thinly sliced green onions  garlic  soy sauce nor seasoning  sugar  black pepper and a large egg that's been  lightly beaten we recommend using a fork  to mix everything together to avoid over  mixing over mixing or filling can make  the meat tough then cover the bowl in  plastic wrap and pop it in the fridge to  chill for 1 hour  time to talk wrappers you can make your  own lumpia wrappers if you have the time  but store-bought lumpia wrappers from  your local filipino or asian market are  great options and more convenient lumpia  wrappers are often referred to as spring  roll wrappers but don't confuse them  with fresh spring roll wrappers fresh  spring roll wrappers or rice paper are  thin like lumpia wrappers but they're  clear and typically not cooked we  recommend using the standard traditional  9 inch round loom pair wrappers  be sure to pull all the wrappers apart  before you start rolling this will help  speed up the assembly process to keep  your wrappers from drying out cover them  with a slightly damp towel or plastic  wrap so they don't dry out if you don't  do this the wrappers will fall apart as  you roll them  the last thing we need to do is make a  slurry  this acts as a glue and keeps our lumpia  intact when it's fried egg wash also  works we did a test with just water but  found that the wrappers unraveled to  make the slurry combine a bit of flour  with water and now we're ready for the  fun part place a wrapper on a large work  surface and spoon five tablespoons of  the filling inside you might think the  more filling the better here but it's a  delicate balance the wrapper and the  filling have to complement and support  each other like a pie crust to its apple  filling and corn tortilla to its babil  now when it comes to rolling it's a lot  like rolling a cigar or a cigarette fold  the bottom half of the wrapper on top of  the filling and push and tuck the top  half onto the filling roll the filling  forward away from you then apply the  slurry onto the top edge of the wrapper  and roll completely to seal and we did  it it's that simple in most filipino  households you'll get an army of people  to help you with this part  so recruit friends or family when you're  making these  we promise they'll love it once rolling  is complete we would like to cut them  into smaller pieces  we found they are easier to fry this way  but you can definitely leave them long  either way they'll taste great now a lot  of families like to freeze them to fry  later for a party or a quick dinner or  snack to do this set them on a sheet  tray in a single layer not touching and  freeze for about two to three hours once  frozen  transfer them to a sealed container and  you can store them in the freezer for  three to four weeks max however we want  to eat them now so it's time to fry in a  heavy bottom pot pour canola oil until  it reaches one inch high and heat until  reaches 375 degrees fahrenheit once it's  hot enough gently place some lumpia in  the oil don't overcrowd the pan you want  to make sure they cook evenly let them  fry for about five to seven minutes you  know they are ready when they look like  this perfectly golden brown  set them on a cooling rack lined with  paper towels to absorb any excess oil  bring the oil back up to 375 degrees  fahrenheit to ensure they get just as  crispy man this smells like our family  birthday parties when i was growing up  we'll keep doing this until all our  lumpia is fried  when it comes time to serve lumpia would  not be complete without a dipping sauce  lumpy on shanghai is often paired with  something that is both sweet and tangy  like banana ketchup sweet chili sauce or  sweet and sour sauce so pick whatever is  your favorite and it's time to dig in  grab one of those flaky rolls of  goodness  generously dip it in your sauce and  enjoy we hope you give this recipe a try  and let us know in the comments what  your favorite kind of lumpia is to find  this recipe and many more visit  tasty.com  oh yes  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

